arXiv:2602.22453v1 [cs.CL] 25 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shaswat Patel
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal contribution.</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author: spp9399@nyu.edu and vt2369@nyu.edu</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vishvesh Trivedi<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        </span></span></span><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
          
          
          
        </span></span></span>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yue Han
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yihuai Hong
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Eunsol Choi
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>New York University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has identified a subset of attention heads in Transformer as retrieval heads, which are responsible for retrieving information from the context. In this work, we first investigate retrieval heads in multilingual contexts. In multilingual language models, we find that retrieval heads are often shared across multiple languages. Expanding the study to cross-lingual setting, we identify <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Retrieval-Transition heads (RTH)</span>, which govern the transition to specific target-language output.
Our experiments reveal that RTHs are distinct from retrieval heads and more vital for Chain-of-Thought reasoning in multilingual LLMs. Across four multilingual benchmarks (MMLU-ProX, MGSM, MLQA, and XQuaD) and two model families (Qwen-2.5 and Llama-3.1), we demonstrate that masking RTH induces bigger performance drop than masking Retrieval Heads (RH).
Our work advances understanding of multilingual LMs by isolating the attention heads responsible for mapping to target languages.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved state-of-the-art performance in reasoning and knowledge-intensive tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al. (2025)</a></cite>. As LLMs are deployed globally, their ability to retrieve, reason, and respond across multiple languages became crucial. Recent research has linked specific behaviors to internal circuits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ferrando et al. (2024)</a></cite>, notably identifying <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">retrieval heads</span> (RH) as the sparse subset of attention heads responsible for extracting facts from long contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we investigate multilingual retrieval by extending standard Needle-In-A-Haystack (NIAH) analysis to multiple languages. Evaluating three model families across English, German, Chinese, and Swahili, we find a consistent mixture of language-shared and language-exclusive retrieval heads. For instance, Qwen2.5-7B exhibits 59% overlap across all four languages, while Phi-3.5 shows significantly higher language-specific specialization with 21% <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en</span>-only heads (§<a href="#S3" title="3 Retrieval Heads in Diverse Languages ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). We observe a rough correlation between model pretraining corpus and the shared <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">linguistic</span> nature of its retrieval head, similar to analogous findings for MLP heads <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Multilingual proficiency requires more than "copy-paste" retrieval; models must translate retrieved evidence into target-language output.
<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Multilingual models are observed to reason within a English-centric latent space, independent of the specific language used for final generation. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wendler et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</span> This raises a critical question: <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">what enables the transition from language-agnostic reasoning to language-specific token generation?</span> We define Retrieval-Transition Heads (RTH), by measuring whether a head’s attention aligns target-language output tokens with their corresponding source-language "needle" counterparts (§<a href="#S4" title="4 Retrieval-Transition heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finally, through causal interventions across two model families (<span id="S1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen-2.5</span> and <span id="S1.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama-3.1</span>) and four benchmarks, we establish that RTHs are functionally indispensable for multilingual reasoning. We find that masking a sparse set of RTHs triggers a catastrophic performance collapse—most notably a 54-point drop in MGSM for Llama-3.1—over 3<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> the drop seen in standard RH (§<a href="#S5" title="5 Evaluation of Retrieval-Transition Heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)</span>. Qualitatively, RTH masking spikes loss of language coherence when compared to RH masking which predominately causes retrieval failures (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 The role of retrieval-transition heads in multilingual reasoning performance ‣ 5 Evaluation of Retrieval-Transition Heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). These results characterize RTHs as a critical, language-agnostic bottleneck for the internal reasoning mechanisms of LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze retrieval heads in multilingual LLMs, finding that they decompose into language-shared and language-exclusive subsets, with significant variance across model families.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the Retrieval-Transition Score to measure cross-lingual correspondence at the head level and characterize the Retrieval-Transition Heads responsible for mapping retrieved evidence to target-language synthesis.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through masking experiments on several multilingual reasoning benchmarks we causally prove the role of RTH for CoT reasoning in multilingual LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Role of attention heads</span> Various studies have proposed techniques to explain Multi-Head Attention (MHA) in Transformer architectures and discovered distinct functions performed by a certain set of attention heads <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gould et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDougall et al. (2024)</a></cite> studies copy suppression, the functionality of the attention head to reduce the prediction of a token that has appeared previously in the context. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olsson et al. (2022)</a></cite> discovered <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Induction heads</span>, which implement a "match and copy" mechanism to complete patterns found in the preceding context. They observe that emergence of Induction Heads coincides with sudden improvement in in-context learning abilities of the models. Most relevant to our work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite> identify <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Retrieval Heads</span> as the primary drivers of long-form in-context learning and retrieval. They note that masking the 50 most influential retrieval heads leads up to 50% drop in performance tasks requiring Chain-of-Thought reasoning. This discovery has enabled practical applications, such as head-level KV cache compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. (2025)</a></cite> and improving contextual faithfulness in long-horizon reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Understanding Multilingual Language Models</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite a English-centric bias in training data, MLMs demonstrate surprisingly good multilingual proficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wendler et al. (2024a)</a></cite> investigated this via logit lens and causal interventions. They show that while the initial and final layers operate in the input/output language, the middle layers transition into an English-centric but latent concept space.
Following this study, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite> explored cross-lingual inconsistencies, where models successfully retrieve parametric facts when prompted in English but fail in other languages. They attribute failure in mapping language-agnostic concepts back into the specific non-English token space. Recent work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite> reports that as models scale and are trained more multi-lingually, they move away from English-centric behaviour and develop a core language-agnostic parameter space composed of MLP heads termed as "shared neurons". Further, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> reported that MLMs share circuits for identical syntactic processes while employing distinct attention heads and feed-forward layers for language specific linguistic processes. In our work we investigate whether retrieval heads, the backbone of complex reasoning, are similarly partitioned and compare how this attribute varies with model families.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attention heads in multilingual LLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have localized language-specific heads and demonstrated how causal interventions can shift a model’s language focus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a></cite> or trigger direct translation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anonymous (2025)</a></cite>. While we share the goal of identifying heads responsible for multilingual performance, prior efforts focus largely on translation or short-context QA. These approaches offer limited insight into modern workflows requiring sustained coherence across long Chain-of-Thought (CoT) sequences. We introduce Retrieval-Transition Heads (RTH), which are analogous to standard retrieval heads <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite> but specialized for multilingual contexts. Crucially, whereas previous work attributes performance drops to incorrect output language (language-shifting), we demonstrate that RTH masking causes failures in linguistic coherence and context grounding even when the model attempts to answer in the correct target language.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Retrieval Heads in Diverse Languages</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Background</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify retrieval heads for different languages using the Needle-In-A-Haystack (NIAH) task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamradt (2023)</a></cite> following the same experimental setup as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For completeness, we describe the process below.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Needle-In-A-Haystack:</span> NIAH task consists of a tuple <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,q,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,q,k)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the long-context text (a.k.a. haystack), <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the question and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the answer related to the question (a.k.a. needle). Generally, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is framed so that the model has no parametric knowledge about the answer and has to retrieve <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to answer the question. NIAH showcases a model’s ability to accurately answer questions based on a long context. In our study, we further extend NIAH to various languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval score (RS): </span> Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we calculate an attention head’s retrieval score by considering the proportion of decoded tokens part of the needle sentence where the head assigned the most attention weight. Concretely, we define the score for an attention head per example <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{h}=\sum_{t=1}^{N}\mathcal{N}^{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{h}=\sum_{t=1}^{N}\mathcal{N}^{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{where,}\quad\mathcal{N}^{t}=\begin{cases}\dfrac{1}{N},&amp;\text{if }\operatorname*{arg\,max}(a_{h}^{t})\in k\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>where,</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{where,}\quad\mathcal{N}^{t}=\begin{cases}\dfrac{1}{N},&amp;\text{if }\operatorname*{arg\,max}(a_{h}^{t})\in k\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is needle length, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{h}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{h}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the attention assigned to each input token by head <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> at decoding timestep <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the output token that is retrieved from the needle sentence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using retrieval scores associated with attention head we can quantify which heads illustrate copy-paste behavior for different languages. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Multilingual Experimental Setting</h3>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22453v1/Graphics/appendix_figs/sectionA/NIAH_generation.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/124;" width="476" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Multilingual NIAH dataset construction pipeline. We sample needles, questions and haystacks, translate the needle and question into the target language, segment the haystack, and inject the translated needle to produce the final evaluation instances.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<br class="ltx_break">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multilingual NIAH data construction</span> We selected English (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en</span>), German (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">de</span>), Chinese (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">zh</span>), and Swahili (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sw</span>) to analyze retrieval behavior across diverse linguistic typologies and pretraining resource levels. <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">English and German were included to observe shared behaviors between closely related high-resource languages. Chinese provides a typologically distinct high-resource counterpoint, while Swahili represents a low-resource setting to investigate the impact of token scarcity on retrieval mechanisms.</span> We further validate these observations by extending our analysis to Arabic (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ar</span>), Dutch (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl</span>), and Hindi (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hi</span>) in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Considering the effect of including more low-resource languages ‣ Appendix A Effect of model sizes, architecture, and low-resource languages to retrieval head analysis. ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As seen in Figure  <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2 Multilingual Experimental Setting ‣ 3 Retrieval Heads in Diverse Languages ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, to extend the NIAH task for different languages, we first create a haystack using Wikipedia dumps of each language.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Database_download" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Database_download</a></span></span></span> To create the needle, we use Google Translate<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://translate.google.com/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://translate.google.com/</a></span></span></span> to translate the same needles as used by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite> to ensure consistency of evaluation. We then segment the haystack to match the desired context length and insert the needle at a controlled depth. This pipeline is fully automatic and readily scalable to other languages. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we evaluate 600 examples, spanning <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3 needles,</span> 20 context-length settings and 10 needle-depth percentages, and report each head’s mean score averaged over all configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models</span>
We evaluate on three open-source model families: Qwen2.5 7B Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Phi3.5 3B MiniInstruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and Llama-3.1 8B Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite>.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Given all are Instruct models, we omit the use of words ’Instruct’ &amp; ’MiniInstruct’ for better readability.</span></span></span> We choose these models to span widely-used open-source multilingual LLMs with diverse architectures and training recipes.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.22453v1/Graphics/fig1_treemaps/Qwen_2.5_7B_EN_ZH_DE_SW_head_interesection.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/134;" width="229" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.22453v1/Graphics/fig1_treemaps/Phi_3.5_3B_Mini_Instruct_EN_ZH_DE_SW_head_interesection.png" id="S3.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/134;" width="229" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.22453v1/Graphics/fig1_treemaps/LLaMa3.1_8B_Instruct_EN_ZH_DE_SW_head_interesection.png" id="S3.F2.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/134;" width="229" height="134" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22453v1/treemap_table.svg" id="S3.F2.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/95;" width="229" height="95"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Language-specific (red) and shared (orange, green, blue) retrieval heads in three multilingual LMs for four languages - English (<span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en</span>), Chinese (<span id="S3.F2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">zh</span>), German (<span id="S3.F2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">de</span>), Swahili (<span id="S3.F2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sw</span>). The size of each block is proportional to the number of retrieval heads that are language-exclusive (red), bilingual (orange), trilingual (green), or language-shared (blue). Qwen-2.5 7B exhibits strong multilingual head overlap (59% 4L), Llama-3.1 8B has high bilingual (mainly <span id="S3.F2.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en</span>-<span id="S3.F2.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">zh</span>) head overlap (24.6% 2L), and Phi-3.5 3B shows a measurably higher language-specific (mainly <span id="S3.F2.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en</span>-specific) behavior (28.4% 1L) compared to the other two model families.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Analyzing distribution of retrieval heads across different languages</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We find that retrieval heads also exhibit a mixture of language-shared and language-exclusive heads, consistent with patterns discovered in recent multilingual studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>. However, from Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Multilingual Experimental Setting ‣ 3 Retrieval Heads in Diverse Languages ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we report a considerable variance of retrieval head intersection across four languages - English, German, Chinese, and Swahili for different model families. For instance, 118 of 200 (59.0%) heads in Qwen2.5-7B are shared among all four languages while 94 of 211 heads (44.5%) in Phi3.5-3B are shared. Moreover, the proportion of bilingual <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en-zh</span> heads in Llama-3.1 8B accounts for 19% of all retrieval heads, more than twice the most abundant bilingual pairs in Qwen-2.5 7B (8.0% <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en-zh</span>) and Phi-3.5 3B (8.1% <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en-sw</span>). Finally, it is observed that Phi3.5-3B exhibits a disproportionately high number of <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en</span>-specific retrieval heads (21.8%), while other models like Qwen-2.5 7B and Llama-3.1 8B have lower counts for <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en</span>-specific heads (6.0% and 10.2% respectively). We attribute this English-inclined behaviour of Phi-3.5 3B to its small model size and a different synthetic post-training recipe, as further discussed in Appendix Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table A1 ‣ Appendix B Language Specificity and Training Data Composition ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>. Our observations are consistent across increasing model scales and varying architectures (refer Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Effect of scaling model size on Qwen-2.5 ‣ Appendix A Effect of model sizes, architecture, and low-resource languages to retrieval head analysis. ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> and <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Comparing Mixture-of-Experts to fully activated model architecture on Phi-3.5 ‣ Appendix A Effect of model sizes, architecture, and low-resource languages to retrieval head analysis. ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> respectively) suggesting an invariance to these factors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Retrieval-Transition heads</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While retrieval heads facilitate reasoning by copying information from the previous context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, this mechanism alone is insufficient for LLMs trained on multiple languages. <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Because MLMs operate within a target-language-agnostic concept space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wendler et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, they must do more than just copy</span>; they must translate latent, abstract thoughts into specific target-language tokens. Modifying experimental setup from standard retrieval head detection, we identify distinct heads, which we name as <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Retrieval-Transition heads (RTH)</span> that perform this mapping behavior.
Consequently, we show that masking these heads causes a more severe drop in CoT performance than standard retrieval heads (RH), as the model is impaired from its ability to verbalize latent thoughts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22453v1/Graphics/retrieval_translation_heads/retrieval_translation_head_new.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/236;" width="476" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Illustration of the retrieval-transition score (RTS). Needle tokens in the source language are first aligned to their target-language equivalents using a LM. The degree of overlap between the model’s generated tokens and these aligned target needles quantifies each attention head’s contribution to cross-lingual retrieval.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-Lingual NIAH Task:</span> <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We use English as a pivot language to approximate the concept space as the latent token vocabulary is not directly accessible.</span> However, we demonstrate that this choice is functionally arbitrary and depends solely on which language most closely aligns with the model’s internal representations (c.f. Figure 4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite>). In models with more balanced multilingual training (such as the Qwen-2.5 family) we utilize Chinese as the proxy for the concept space to derive retrieval-transition heads for English. Our experiments show that masking the identified heads leads to a significant performance collapse even in English-language CoT tasks, confirming that these heads are essential for the transition from the latent space to any specific target language. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Retrieval-Transition heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we introduce a modified variant of the NIAH task, where the haystack and needle are expressed in a language that approximates the concept space <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{src}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{src}</annotation></semantics></math> while the model is prompted to generate the output in a given target language <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{tgt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{tgt}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval transition score:</span> To identify attention heads that facilitate retrieval across linguistic boundaries, we extend the standard retrieval score (RS) formulation to account for cross-token correspondence. At each decoding timestep <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, this requires establishing a semantic mapping between the generated output token in the target language <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{tgt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{tgt}</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding "needle" token in the source context <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{src}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{src}</annotation></semantics></math>. We utilize <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-30B-A3B-Thinking-2507<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">4</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-30B-A3B-Thinking-2507" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url">https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-30B-A3B-Thinking-2507</a></span></span></span></span> to perform this alignment, ensuring that output tokens are correctly mapped to their source-language counterparts. We also evaluate an alternative LLM-based aligner and find that the identified retrieval-transition heads are largely stable to the choice of aligner (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4 Analyzing variability introduced by LLM-based aligner ‣ Appendix C Characteristics of Retrieval Transition Heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>). The Retrieval Transition Score for a given attention head <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(RTS_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(RTS_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTS_{h}=\sum_{t=1}^{N}\mathcal{A}^{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTS_{h}=\sum_{t=1}^{N}\mathcal{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{where }\mathcal{A}^{t}=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{N},&amp;\text{if }\operatorname{argmax}(a_{h}^{t})\in f^{-1}(\text{token}_{t})\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>argmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>token</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{where }\mathcal{A}^{t}=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{N},&amp;\text{if }\operatorname{argmax}(a_{h}^{t})\in f^{-1}(\text{token}_{t})\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{N}_{src}\to\mathcal{N}_{tgt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{N}_{src}\to\mathcal{N}_{tgt}</annotation></semantics></math> is the alignment function mapping source-language needle tokens to their target-language equivalents, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{t}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{t}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the attention distribution of head <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> at decoding timestep <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(\text{token}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>token</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(\text{token}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of source tokens corresponding to the current target token being decoded.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22453v1/Graphics/section4/layer_RH_vs_RTH_Qwen.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/187;" width="476" height="187" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Layer–head distributions of Retrieval Heads (RH) and Retrieval-Transition Heads (RTH) across languages for Qwen-2.5 7B Instruct. Colored cells are part of Top-50 most prominent heads ranked by score. Among these colored heads, we find language-specific RH are dominant in the final layers while RTH are prominent in the middle layers. Like RH heads, RTH heads too show <span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sparsity</span> with only 3-8% of heads with an RTS score above 0.1</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22453v1/Graphics/section4/Qwen_rh_vs_rth_heads_t50.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/389;" width="476" height="389" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Spearman correlation score between top-50 most influential <math id="S4.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTH(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTH(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> in Qwen-2.5 7B. <math id="S4.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTH(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTH(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> heads share little correlation with <math id="S4.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> but high correlations among other <math id="S4.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTH(\ell_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTH(\ell_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and vice-versa</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Language</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Top-15</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Top-25</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Top-50</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Top-100</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">EN</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">10</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">26</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">63</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">DE</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">8</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">28</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">71</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">ZH</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">6</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">10</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">30</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">62</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">SW</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">10</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">27</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">57</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Number of intersecting top-<math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> heads <math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTH(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTH(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> for each language <math id="S4.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. We find minimal overlap especially among the most influential <math id="S4.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTH(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTH(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span>suggesting their different functions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models and Languages evaluated:</span> Same as Retrieval Head analysis (c.f. § <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Multilingual Experimental Setting ‣ 3 Retrieval Heads in Diverse Languages ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) we perform our experiments on Qwen2.5 7B Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Phi3.5 3B MiniInstruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and Llama-3.1 8B Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite> with English (<span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">en</span>), German (<span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">de</span>), Chinese (<span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">zh</span>), and Swahili (<span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sw</span>) as target languages (<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{tgt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{tgt}</annotation></semantics></math>). We use English as the source language (<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{src}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{src}</annotation></semantics></math>) for all experiments except for <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTH_{en}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTH_{en}</annotation></semantics></math> where we use Chinese.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22453v1/Graphics/section4/qwen_llama_baseline_as_bar.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/123;" width="476" height="123" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of attention head masking (<math id="S4.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=25</annotation></semantics></math>) on downstream multilingual reasoning performance.</span> We compare the effects of masking random heads, Retrieval Heads (RH), and Retrieval-Transition Heads (RTH) on two models (Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B) averaged across languages. While random masking has a negligible effect, masking RTHs (red) leads to a significantly more severe collapse in accuracy compared to standard RHs (orange), particularly on reasoning-heavy tasks like MGSM.</figcaption>
</figure>
