<td id="S4.T2.3.1.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;">18.8 <math id="S4.T2.m179" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 2.48<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.9.5.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.9.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-29.2</span></sup></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.9.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B32121;">-21.80</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.9.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;">34.10 <math id="S4.T2.m181" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.31<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.9.7.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.9.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-4.95</span></sup></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.9.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;">44.25 <math id="S4.T2.m183" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.48<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.9.8.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.9.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-5.24</span></sup></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.9.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;">32.49 <math id="S4.T2.m185" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.51<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.9.9.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.9.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-4.27</span></sup></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.9.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.9.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B32121;">-4.82</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.3.1.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.3.1.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">RTH</span></th>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">3.6 <math id="S4.T2.m187" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;" mathbackground="#E6F2FF">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 1.18<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.10.2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-69.6</span></sup></span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">5.6 <math id="S4.T2.m189" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;" mathbackground="#E6F2FF">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 1.46<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.10.3.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-56.8</span></sup></span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">15.2 <math id="S4.T2.m191" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;" mathbackground="#E6F2FF">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 2.28<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.10.4.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-44.0</span></sup></span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">2.4 <math id="S4.T2.m193" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;" mathbackground="#E6F2FF">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.97<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.10.5.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-45.6</span></sup></span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B32121;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">-54.00</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">21.90 <math id="S4.T2.m195" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;" mathbackground="#E6F2FF">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.26<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.10.7.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-17.15</span></sup></span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">38.92 <math id="S4.T2.m197" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;" mathbackground="#E6F2FF">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.48<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.10.8.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-10.57</span></sup></span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">34.41 <math id="S4.T2.m199" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;" mathbackground="#E6F2FF">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.53<sup id="S4.T2.3.1.10.9.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-2.35</span></sup></span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.10.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;padding:1.5pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.10.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B32121;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F2FF;">-10.02</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-language performance degradation under top-<math id="S4.T2.m204" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.T2.m205" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=25</annotation></semantics></math>) head masking.</span> Accuracy (MMLU-ProX, MGSM) and F1-scores (MLQA, XQuaD) are reported for Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B. <math id="S4.T2.m206" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{Avg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{Avg}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the average drop relative to the no-masking baseline. Results highlight that RTH masking consistently induces the largest performance drops across high-resource (en, zh, de) and often in low-resource (sw) languages. Crucially, English-language tasks are also affected when masking RTH, suggesting that the latent reasoning mechanism in multilingual language models is invariant to the target language.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Evaluation of Retrieval-Transition Heads</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>The distribution of retrieval-transition heads</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Retrieval-Transition heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (left), we report the location of retrieval-transition heads in comparison to the location of retrieval heads. We observe that RT and RTH exhibit considerable degree of overlap. However, as seen in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Retrieval-Transition heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the intersection between the two head groups is notably low for most prominent heads in both sets; for instance, only 2 out of the top 15 heads are common to both categories in English, German, and Swahili. Even as the search space expands to the top 50 heads, the overlap remains limited to approximately half of the heads.
Among the intersecting heads, we examine the Spearman rank correlation for top-50 most prominent heads in Qwen 2.5 7B Instruct for RH and RTH across four languages: English (en), Chinese (zh), German (de), and Swahili (sw). As illustrated in the global correlation matrix (Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Retrieval-Transition heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), consistent clusters emerges within RH heads, and within RTH heads where coefficients remain high, typically ranging from 0.66 to 0.91. However, when comparing RTH with RH, even for the same language, we observe little to negative correlation. Moreover, most prominent RTHs appear predominantly in the middle layers (14-23 in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Retrieval-Transition heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) while language-specific RHs appear mainly in the final layers (18 of top-50 heads in last 3 layers) in Qwen 2.5 7B Instruct. <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">As shown in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Characteristics of Retrieval Transition Heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, RTH characteristics are consistent across various model families, scales, and training recipes.</span>. These results suggest that while within-language retrieval is unified, the transition from latent concepts to target languages relies on distinct, specialized circuits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>The role of retrieval-transition heads in multilingual reasoning performance</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To understand the role of retrieval-transition heads in reasoning performance, we perform head masking experiments on 4 multilingual benchmarks (subject reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xuan et al. (2025)</a></cite>, math reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and extractive question-answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Artetxe et al. (2020)</a></cite>). MMLU-ProX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xuan et al. (2025)</a></cite> is a multilingual, reasoning-centric multiple-choice QA benchmark that requires complex, multi-step reasoning and covers 29 languages. MGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2022)</a></cite> is an expertly annotated multilingual extension of the GSM-8k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al. (2021)</a></cite> dataset and consists of arithmetic word problems to evaluate numerical and logical reasoning across multiple languages while controlling for problem structure. MLQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al. (2020)</a></cite> is a multilingual extractive question answering benchmark that tests cross-lingual reading comprehension by requiring models to locate answer spans in passages written in different languages. XQuaD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Artetxe et al. (2020)</a></cite> is a cross-lingual QA dataset designed to measure zero-shot transfer, where models trained on English must answer extractive questions in unseen target languages. We evaluate each language under a monolingual protocol, where the question and answer options are presented exclusively in that language.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we quantify the causal impact of specific heads by perturbing their pre-softmax attention logits during the forward pass; a formal description of this intervention is provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Causal Intervention through Attention Head Masking ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</span> For each language <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, we evaluate three masking strategies: (i) masking the top-<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">k</span> randomly selected heads, (ii) masking the top-<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">k</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (iii) masking the top-<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">k</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTH_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTH_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. In all experiments, we set <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{k}=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">k</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{k}=25</annotation></semantics></math> and use chain-of-thought prompt template <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a></cite>. <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We describe our results on two models - Qwen-2.5 7B Instruct and Llama-3.1 8B Instruct and additionally provide results by varying hyperparameter <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Effect of hyperparameter k (number of heads masked) ‣ Appendix E Additional experiments and Qualitative analysis of Retrieval-Transition Heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a></span>. Our results are consistent with different values of <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, however, with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 50</annotation></semantics></math> we observe that retrieval failures—missing relevant information from the question—drives most of the degradation by disrupting the model’s chain-of-thought, which largely overwhelms the effect of weakening the transition from latent representations to language-specific decoding. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Retrieval-Transition heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> summarizes the effect of masking different attention head sets, averaged across languages, for the Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct models across the MMLU-ProX, MGSM, MLQA, and XQuaD benchmarks. We observe that masking <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=25</annotation></semantics></math> random attention heads has a negligible effect on downstream performance, while masking an equivalent number of Retrieval Heads (RH) leads to a measurable decline. However, the most drastic performance collapse across all datasets occurs when masking Retrieval-Transition Heads (RTH). A striking example is found in mathematical reasoning (MGSM), where performance drops by 54 points for Llama-3.1 and 17 points for Qwen-2.5.</span> Overall, these aggregate results indicate that retrieval-transition heads are significantly more critical than standard retrieval heads for maintaining multilingual reasoning capabilities. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Retrieval-Transition heads ‣ Bridging Latent Reasoning and Target-Language Generation via Retrieval-Transition Heads" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a granular, per-language breakdown across the four benchmarks. Aside from Swahili (<span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sw</span>) in MMLU-ProX, the disparity in performance loss between RH and RTH masking is statistically significant across all languages. The outlier behavior in Swahili is expected given its low-resource nature, where the model generally struggles with even basic, copy-paste retrieval. Notably, masking RTHs triggers a severe collapse even in English-language tasks, far exceeding the degradation seen when masking RHs or random heads. These results experimentally confirm the existence of a language-transition mechanism in multilingual LLMs and support recent findings regarding the latent, target-language independent nature of this internal transition logic.</span></p>
</div>
